Queue and deque.

Some collections aren't so much about storing elements, as about processing them in an order. A queue hands elements back in a disciplined sequence — usually first-in, first-out, like a line at a counter. A deque — spelled d-e-q-u-e, and pronounced "deck", for double-ended queue — is the more general tool: you can add and remove at both ends, which lets one type serve as a queue and as a stack.

Take the queue first, for first-in-first-out processing. A queue has a front, where you remove, and a back, where you add. Its methods come in two flavours — one that throws on failure, and one that signals with a special value — and you should prefer the signalling forms. You make an array-deque. Offer adds at the back — while add would throw if the queue were full. Peek looks at the front without removing it, giving you the first task, or null if it's empty. And poll removes and returns the front, or null if empty. So offer, poll, and peek return null or false on an empty or full queue, whereas add, remove, and element throw. For everyday code, the non-throwing trio is safer. Queues model work pipelines, breadth-first traversal, and buffering between a producer and a consumer.

A deque adds the other end, with explicit first and last methods — add-first and add-last, peek-first and peek-last, poll-first and poll-last. And because you can push and pop at just one end, a deque is a stack — last-in, first-out. It even provides push, pop, and peek names for exactly that. So a browser history is an array-deque: you push a page, which is add-first, and pop to go back, which is remove-first, in last-in-first-out order.

The one implementation to remember is array-deque. It's the standard, high-performance implementation of both queue and deque — a resizable circular array, constant time at both ends, and cache-friendly. It's the right default for both a queue and a stack. There are two legacy classes for the same jobs, and you should avoid both. Stack is an ancient class that extends Vector, synchronized on every operation, with a confused A-P-I — so use array-deque as your stack, never Stack. And linked-list implements deque too, but with node overhead and poor cache behaviour, so again, prefer array-deque. One caveat: array-deque forbids null elements, because it uses null internally to mean "empty" — so if you genuinely must store nulls, that's the rare exception.

Finally, a different discipline: the priority-queue. It always hands back the smallest element next — by natural order, or by a comparator — not the oldest. It's a binary heap, so offer and poll are order log-n. You offer five, one, and three, and poll returns one, the minimum, regardless of insertion order. Reach for it whenever you want to always process the highest-priority item: schedulers, Dijkstra's algorithm, or top-k problems.

So the rule of thumb: array-deque for first-in-first-out queues and for stacks, and priority-queue when order-by-priority matters. With the four collection shapes now covered, the rest of this module is the skills that cut across all of them — starting with how to iterate them safely.
